    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d381">

                <pb facs="0588" n="542" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg542"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/15630½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cornish, N. H.</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1915-08-01">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">1 August, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I am very glad of this,
                    and write to ask whether you think it would be best for me to be in Washington
                    at that time or to wait until the deliberations and actions of this group seem
                    to be approaching a point where they can offer practical advice. I should like
                    to do what will best assist psychologically as well as practically.</p>
                <p>I think that it will be wise to announce at once the approach of this conference
                    and its objects, in general terms, not too fully disclosing what we wish to
                    leave a little vague, viz. just what we expect the procedure and the action of
                    the U. S. towards what the conference advises to be.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
